        "Shinji Yuasa",
        "Stuart S. P. Parkin",
        "William H. Butler"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Giant tunnel magnetoresistance established a central spin-dependent transport effect and enabled major advances in magnetic storage and spintronics.",
        "attribution": "Yuasa and Parkin made landmark experimental demonstrations; Butler supplied influential theoretical understanding. The listed names capture key experimental and theoretical contributions, though the broader history includes other groups.",
        "maturity": "The effect is thoroughly confirmed and has had durable technological impact.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The relative priority and credit among the pioneering experimental groups require careful historical assessment."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B022",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Kane",
        "Eugene J. Mele",
        "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Topological insulators and the quantum spin Hall effect revealed a robust new class of electronic states and reshaped condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele developed the seminal theoretical framework; Molenkamp's experiments established the quantum spin Hall state in a material. This is a strong theory–experiment pairing.",
        "maturity": "The foundational predictions and observations are well established, with many confirmed materials and follow-on discoveries.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The scope of a prize would need to distinguish this foundational discovery from later topological phases."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B025",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Akira Ohtomo",
        "Harold Y. Hwang",
        "Jochen Mannhart"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The discovery of a conducting two-dimensional electron gas at an oxide interface opened a rich field of interfacial electronic phases and control.",
        "attribution": "Ohtomo and Hwang's interface work is foundational, and Mannhart's contributions helped establish oxide heterostructures as a platform. Credit should reflect the rapid contributions of other groups.",
        "maturity": "The interfacial conductivity is well confirmed, although proposed emergent phases and explanations vary in robustness.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The prize case depends on defining the discovery narrowly enough to separate established results from debated interpretations."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B041",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christian Pfleiderer",
        "Roland Wiesendanger",
        "Yoshinori Tokura"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magnetic skyrmions established nanoscale topological spin textures as real, controllable states of matter and created a promising direction for spintronics.",
        "attribution": "Pfleiderer and colleagues identified skyrmion phases in chiral magnets, while Tokura's work broadened materials and physical understanding; Wiesendanger contributed influential imaging and nanoscale studies.",
        "maturity": "Skyrmions have been observed and manipulated in several systems, but their practical advantage for devices remains unsettled.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Priority and the division of credit between theoretical prediction, bulk observation, and imaging are not straightforward."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hideo Hosono",
        "Toshio Kamiya"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Transparent amorphous oxide semiconductors combined optical transparency with useful electronic transport and enabled widely deployed display technology.",
        "attribution": "Hosono's materials discoveries and Kamiya's work on electronic properties and devices form a credible core attribution.",
        "maturity": "The materials and their technological use are well established, giving this work an unusually clear record of application.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The broad development of oxide semiconductor devices involved substantial contributions beyond the two listed names."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Allan H. MacDonald",
        "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero",
        "Rafi Bistritzer"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene revealed tunable correlated and superconducting states in a clean, adjustable two-dimensional system.",
        "attribution": "Bistritzer and MacDonald predicted the moiré flat-band mechanism; Jarillo-Herrero's group demonstrated correlated insulating and superconducting behavior. This theory–experiment link is compelling.",
        "maturity": "The principal observations have been reproduced and the field is mature enough for recognition, though the microscopic origins of several phases remain active questions.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The mechanism of superconductivity and the relation among observed phases remain incompletely settled."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B046",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory made a major conceptual and practical advance in describing strongly correlated electrons, including metal–insulator transitions.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar are central contributors to the method and its development; the method arose through a broader theoretical community.",
        "maturity": "The framework is extensively used and has been tested across many material problems, although its approximations limit some applications.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The case for a physics prize depends on treating a powerful theoretical method as a discovery of comparable standing to a new physical phenomenon."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Jie Shan",
        "Kin Fai Mak",
        "Long Ju"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The fractional quantum anomalous Hall effect extends fractionalized topological physics to moiré materials without an external magnetic field, a potentially landmark advance.",
        "attribution": "Shan, Mak, and Ju are associated with leading experimental realizations; the listed credit should be checked against independent groups and the theoretical foundations.",
        "maturity": "The observations are promising but comparatively recent, and the range of confirmed states and independent replications is still developing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field needs more time to establish reproducibility, the extent of fractional phases, and which experiments merit priority credit."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
